==English==

===Etymology===
Compare Irish and Gaelic {{term|gog}}, a nod, a slight motion.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɡɒ.ɡəl/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɒɡəl|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|goggl|es}}

# to [[stare]] (at something) with [[wide]] [[eye]]s
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Offing|IV, XII, and XV|[...] she frowned a displeased frown and told me for heaven's sake to stop '''goggling''' like a dead halibut. [...]<!--ch. XII--> She left me fogged and groping for the inner meaning, and I could see from Aunt Dahlia's '''goggling''' eyes that the basic idea hadn't got across with her either. <!--ch. XV-->[...] I didn't want to be hampered by an audience. When you're pushing someone into a lake, nothing embarrasses you more than having the front seats filled up with '''goggling''' spectators.}}
# to [[roll]] the eyes
#* ''Hudibras''
#*: And wink and '''goggle''' like an owl.

